WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.580
FedRAMP baselines are grounded in NIST security control concepts,

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.900
then applied to cloud service authorization.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.153
NIST controls provide a structured vocabulary for security and privacy expectations,

00:00:10.153 --> 00:00:12.860
while FedRAMP adds cloud-focused interpretation, documentation expectations,

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.180
assessment practices, and ongoing monitoring context.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.820
This connection helps federal customers and providers discuss controls using a shared

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.687
language instead of inventing a new framework for every service.

00:00:23.687 --> 00:00:28.327
Control families cover broad areas such as access control, audit and accountability,

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:31.420
configuration management, contingency planning, incident response, risk assessment,

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.900
system and communications protection, identification and authentication, maintenance, planning,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.447
and related operational domains.

00:00:36.447 --> 00:00:40.700
The baseline identifies the kinds of control expectations that need attention,

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.340
but the implementation depends on the actual cloud service, architecture, service model,

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.500
and customer responsibilities.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.913
Control implementation statements are where the provider explains how the service satisfies an expectation.

00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:55.780
A strong implementation statement is specific enough to be assessable.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.420
It describes what is done, where it is done, who is responsible,

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.673
what tooling supports it, and what evidence can show it operating.

00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:09.700
Weak statements repeat the control language without showing how the real system works.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.567
Baseline literacy helps teams avoid that hollow style of documentation.

00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:16.273
Cloud shared responsibility makes this more nuanced.

00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:20.140
Some controls may be implemented by the cloud service provider,

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.620
some by an underlying platform, some by corporate services,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.940
and some by the agency customer.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.580
Some controls are inherited from other authorized services or common control providers.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.060
The baseline tells the team what must be addressed;

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:39.860
the architecture and responsibility model determine who does what and how that responsibility is evidenced.
